    <ul>
      <li><strong>Keep the working copy where you work on it.</strong> The practice management system you already use, with its own access controls, is the place for the file. Not the email it arrived on.</li>
      <li><strong>Tidy what's already there.</strong> Search your own mailbox for "passport", "statement" and "P60". What comes back is roughly what someone else would find. Saving those files properly and deleting the emails is an afternoon's work that is worth doing once.</li>
      <li><strong>Tell clients why.</strong> Most of them have never thought about their sent folder. A sentence in your engagement letter goes a long way.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Refueler Share is one way to do the first of these: the file is encrypted in the sender's browser, the link ends when they choose, and nothing lands in anyone's inbox but the link.</p>

    <h2>In short</h2>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data-table data-table-wrap">
        <thead><tr><th>An inbox</th><th>So a client's file</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Keeps everything, with no end date</td><td>Outlives the job it was sent for, in at least two places</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Makes it searchable by one word</td><td>Is easy to find for anyone signed in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Syncs to every device</td><td>Is on every phone and laptop on both sides</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Is the first place looked at after a break-in</td><td>Is exposed along with every other client's</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Email is still the right way to say "Got them, thanks". It just isn't the right place to keep what you got.</p>

